<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Charles IV, Duke of Anjou</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1446">1446</a>–<a href="/years/1481">1481</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Also known as Charles of Maine, Charles du Maine</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Charles IV, Duke of Anjou, also called Charles of Maine, was the nephew and heir of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rene-of-anjou">René of Anjou</a>, whom he succeeded as Count of Provence in 1480. Childless, he bequeathed Provence, Maine and the Angevin claims to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a> to King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xi">Louis XI</a> on his death in 1481. Provence was thereby united with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, and the Naples claim later fuelled the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italian-wars">Italian Wars</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/charles-iv-duke-of-anjou.jpg" alt="Charles IV, Duke of Anjou" width="320" height="374" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Carlodangio · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Arms_of_Charles_v_du_Maine.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
